<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Volhynia</h1><p class="st-meta">1154 CE – 1199 CE (approx.) · capital Volodymyr</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A western Rus' principality around Volodymyr whose prince Roman united it with Galicia in 1199.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Bug</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripyat-river">Pripyat</a>, was an old land of Rus' with its centre at Volodymyr. In the mid 12th century it became the hereditary domain of the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iziaslav-ii-of-kiev">Iziaslav Mstislavich of Kiev</a>, and from 1154 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-ii-of-kiev">Mstislav Iziaslavich</a> ruled it as a separate principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> was a fertile land of towns such as Volodymyr, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Berestia</a>, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the West. Its princes, the senior branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Monomakh</a>'s family, were among the strongest in Rus' and frequently fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a>, who succeeded in 1170, was an energetic warrior who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1199 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Halych</a> after its dynasty died out and joined the two lands into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> later passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-volhynia">Duchy of Volhynia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-volhynia.jpg" alt="Principality of Volhynia" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galicia-Volhynia_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1154 CE – 1199 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Volodymyr</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mstislav Iziaslavich, Roman Mstislavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Volodymyr, Vladimir-Volhynia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1176&amp;focus=principality-of-volhynia" aria-label="Principality of Volhynia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5233 414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Volhynia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5553 670L5697 681M5553 670L5571 720M5553 670L5549 683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M5571 720h0M5549 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Volodymyr. <a href="/atlas?year=1176&amp;focus=principality-of-volhynia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
